WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.680
Stephen King loves "Stranger Things," but&nbsp;
he's got one big complaint about the latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.480
season. What's King's beef with the Duffer&nbsp;
Brothers' latest? Keep watching to find out!

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.320
Warning: "Stranger Things"&nbsp;
Season 4 spoilers ahead.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.120
It's been three long years since&nbsp;
fans last saw some "Stranger Things,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.080
but the show's highly anticipated fourth&nbsp;
season is finally here for all to enjoy.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.840
The show hasn't missed a beat, picking&nbsp;
up with the same level of horror and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.280
intrigue that's gone on to define the series. As&nbsp;
such, it perhaps should come as no surprise to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.200
hear that one of the series' most ardent fans&nbsp;
is the King of Horror himself — Stephen King.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.800
Indeed, the show owes a debt to King and his&nbsp;
impressive body of work, especially when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.640
to Season 4. The series has always maintained&nbsp;
strong roots to King's work, especially seeing how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.240
the series is set in a sleepy, small town, which&nbsp;
has often been the setting of King's stories.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.000
And in the most recent season, there's&nbsp;
a clear homage to King's "Carrie"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.880
when Eleven has a chocolate&nbsp;
milkshake thrown all over her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.440
with parallels to the pig blood scene in&nbsp;
"Carrie." While King seems to love the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.360
he does have one complaint that&nbsp;
appears to be shared by other fans.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.200
"What do you mean, 'like it less'?"

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.520
Like so many other fans have done, King&nbsp;
has taken to social media to voice his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.800
opinion on the most recent "Stranger Things"&nbsp;
season. While he seems primarily smitten with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.920
the latest batch of episodes, he does have one&nbsp;
point he'd like to make. King posted to Twitter,

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.080
"The new season of STRANGER THINGS is really&nbsp;
cool–as good or better than the previous three.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.000
There's even a CARRIE riff. Is it&nbsp;
the whole season or is it another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.960
one of those that's broken into 2&nbsp;
parts? IMHO that's kind of lame."

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.280
"Stranger Things" Season 4 has indeed been split&nbsp;
into two volumes. The first collection came out on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.920
May 27, while the second part is slated to release&nbsp;
on July 1. The second volume will only consist of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:02.080
the final two episodes, the last of which will&nbsp;
clock in at a staggering 2 hours and 30 minutes.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.120
On Twitter, plenty of other people&nbsp;
have voiced their frustration with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.640
having to wait for more "Stranger&nbsp;
Things," with one fan posting,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.360
"Whoever decided to split season 4 of Stranger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.000
Things into 2 parts needs to be put&nbsp;
in jail immediately...IMMEDIATELY!"

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.120
"Seriously?!"

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.360
Another fan wrote,

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.240
"Ugh why [didn't] I know that stranger things last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.560
season was being broken into 2 parts? I&nbsp;
would have waited before I binged it."

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.440
Patience is a virtue, and all of us, including&nbsp;
masters of horror, will just have to wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.000
another month until we know what happens&nbsp;
between the kids from Hawkins and Vecna.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.800
No doubt about it, Netflix has truly changed&nbsp;
the game when it comes to release strategies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.640
For the most part, entire seasons&nbsp;
of shows are dropped on the platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.720
all at once for people to binge-watch.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.080
Lately, the streamer's begun experimenting with&nbsp;
dual volumes, which was the case with the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:57.040
season of "Ozark," and now, it's doing the&nbsp;
same thing with "Stranger Things" Season 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.320
where the first seven episodes came out&nbsp;
initially, followed by the final two on July 1.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.360
Even the length of the episodes, each one&nbsp;
basically being a feature-length movie, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.120
unique in the current television landscape. Season&nbsp;
4 of "Stranger Things" is ambitious in scope in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.480
more ways than one, and according to executive&nbsp;
producer Shawn Levy, there's a very good reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.080
why this dual-release strategy was implemented,&nbsp;
even if it was to the chagrin of Stephen King.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.880
As Levy explained to the Hollywood Reporter,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.640
"We didn't think nine episodes&nbsp;
were going to be ready in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.720
and we have made the world wait long enough.&nbsp;
We always knew that if we could deliver the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.640
first seven, it's such a satisfying&nbsp;
breaking point and pay-off moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.800
that it'll keep people fed for enough&nbsp;
weeks so we can finish up volume two."

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.240
It sounds like the desire to divide Season 4 was&nbsp;
born from necessity and allowed the team behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.880
the scenes to get more time to ensure those&nbsp;
final two episodes were as good as possible.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.280
Levy went on to discuss how streaming allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.240
the team to play around with&nbsp;
their release model, saying,

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.080
"Every streamer is playing around with&nbsp;
different models. We've seen full-season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.680
binge-worthy streaming, aka&nbsp;
'Stranger Things' 1, 2, and 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.880
We've seen Disney+ and Hulu try a weekly&nbsp;
model, which didn't feel quite right for this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.280
So, we're happy that we were able to give&nbsp;
our audience a huge meal of a story."

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.880
Anyone who's finished Episode&nbsp;
7 of "Stranger Things" Season 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.760
is undoubtedly waiting on pins and needles to&nbsp;
see how everything wraps up, and hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.160
the team manages to stick that landing,&nbsp;
and avoid the wrath of the King of Horror.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.360
"I didn't like the ending."

